---
import VerificationBadge from '../components/VerificationBadge.astro';
import ProseLayout from '../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="How this encyclopedia is written"
  kicker="Method"
  lede="Entries are drafted with AI assistance from cited sources, and people review them before they go live. Here is exactly how that works, and how far you can trust a given page."
>
  <h2>From draft to page</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      An AI agent picks the next subject from a public backlog. It researches the subject in sources we allow, and
      it opens every source it cites.
    </li>
    <li>
      It writes the entry to our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>: a fixed article structure,
      traditional and academic views labelled separately, and a citation for every date, attribution, number and
      quotation.
    </li>
    <li>
      Automated checks run on every change. They catch broken links, missing citations, missing sections and
      original-language passages that lack a source.
    </li>
    <li>
      The change is proposed as a pull request. Nothing reaches the site until a person reviews it and merges it.
      The full history of every page is public on <a href={site.repo.url}>GitHub</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="verification">Verification levels</h2>
  <p>Every entry shows one of three badges near its title:</p>
  <dl class="levels">
    <dt><VerificationBadge level="ai-draft" /></dt>
    <dd>
      Written by an AI agent from cited sources. A person approved the change, but has not yet checked every claim
      against its source. Read these entries with care, and check the references for anything that matters to you.
    </dd>
    <dt><VerificationBadge level="human-reviewed" /></dt>
    <dd>A person has checked the entry's claims against the cited sources.</dd>
    <dt><VerificationBadge level="expert-verified" /></dt>
    <dd>
      A named scholar or traditional authority has reviewed the entry. Their name appears beside the badge.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Rules we hold to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No quotation from memory.</strong> Every verse, mantra or quotation is copied from a retrieved edition
      and cited with its edition and locator. Where that isn't possible, we paraphrase and give the reference.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No invented citations.</strong> We cite only sources that were actually consulted.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two views, clearly labelled.</strong> Traditional accounts and academic scholarship are both reported,
      each with its source. Neither is declared the truth in our own voice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fairness between traditions.</strong> No tradition is presented as the real or highest form of
      Sanātana Dharma. A learned practitioner and a careful scholar should both find an entry fair.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A gap is better than an error.</strong> When a claim can't be sourced, we leave it out and list it as
      an open question.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
  <p>
    Every entry has a <em>Report an error</em> link at the bottom. It opens a correction form on GitHub. Scholars
    and practitioners who would like to review entries in their area are warmly invited to
    <a href="/contribute/">get in touch</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>

<style>
  .levels {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    gap: 0.9rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .levels dt {
    padding-top: 0.15rem;
  }

  .levels dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .levels {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
